    <!-- ═══ 06 · FROM INSIGHT TO LIVED CHANGE ═══ -->
    <section class="bg-background">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-3xl ctc-reveal">
        <h2 class="font-heading ctc-gold-text text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[44px] leading-tight mb-10">From insight to lived change</h2>

        <div class="font-body text-lg text-foreground/78 leading-relaxed space-y-7 max-w-[65ch]">
          <p>Understanding ourselves matters, but insight on its own often has surprisingly little power in the moments where it would count most. Someone may know perfectly well that they avoid conflict, choose emotionally unavailable partners, become defensive when they feel exposed, procrastinate when something matters, or lose themselves in other people's needs. The difficulty is rarely recognising the pattern. It is finding what allows a different response while the old one is already unfolding.</p>

          <p>Much of the work therefore stays close to lived experience. We look at what tends to happen just before a familiar reaction, what you feel in the body, which assumptions arrive almost automatically, what the response protects you from, and what it costs you afterwards. The purpose is not to reduce everything to a theory, but to understand a pattern precisely enough that there is somewhere practical to intervene.</p>

          <p>That intervention is often very small: having one conversation differently, noticing the point at which anxiety turns into avoidance, setting a boundary before resentment builds, staying with an emotion rather than escaping into distraction, or testing a belief that has quietly governed your behaviour for years. What happens outside the room then becomes part of the next conversation, including what worked, what did not, what surprised you, and where the real difficulty turned out to be. Over time the process becomes less about collecting insights and more about turning understanding into freedom of action.</p>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="mt-12 md:mt-14 border-l-2 border-primary/50 pl-7 md:pl-8">
          <p class="font-heading ctc-gold-text text-[26px] md:text-[32px] italic leading-snug max-w-[34ch]">The measure of this work is not how someone feels during a session, but how they function in the days and weeks that follow.</p>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ═══ 07 · ABOUT MICHAEL ═══ -->
    <section class="ctc-dark relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background: radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%, hsl(42 40% 30% / 0.1), transparent 55%);"></div>
      <div class="relative container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-4xl ctc-reveal">

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[260px_1fr] gap-10 lg:gap-14 items-start mb-12">
          <div class="max-w-[240px] md:max-w-none mx-auto md:mx-0">
            <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-parchment/12 shadow-xl shadow-black/25 ctc-graded">
              <div class="aspect-[4/5] overflow-hidden">
                <Image src={michaelPortrait} alt="Michael Kaplan" class="w-full h-full object-cover object-center" widths={[400, 520, 640]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 260px, 240px" loading="lazy" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2 class="font-heading text-gold-gradient text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[44px] leading-tight mb-4">About Michael</h2>
            <p class="font-heading text-xl text-parchment/90 mb-1">Michael Kaplan</p>
            <p class="font-body text-sm ctc-gold-light-text tracking-wide leading-relaxed mb-7">BA Psychology &middot; ASCHP-registered Wellness Counsellor (Reg. 10559)</p>
            <div class="font-body text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6 max-w-[62ch]">
              <p>I am an ASCHP-registered Wellness Counsellor with an academic background in psychology and more than a decade of experience in developmental, relational and group work.</p>
              <p>Much of my earlier work was with boys and young men through Boys to Men in Cape Town, where I spent years facilitating group processes around identity, relationships, responsibility, emotional development and the transition into adulthood. During that period I worked under the guidance of clinical psychologist Bernard Altman, whose influence shaped a great deal of how I now think about relationship, honesty and developmental context in work with people.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-gold-light/40 pl-7 md:pl-8 mb-12 max-w-[62ch] md:ml-[300px] lg:ml-[314px]">
          <p class="font-heading text-xl md:text-[23px] text-parchment/85 italic leading-relaxed mb-3">{bernard.data.pullQuote}</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-parchment/60">{bernard.data.name}, {bernard.data.attribution}</p>
        </blockquote>

        <div class="font-body text-lg leading-relaxed space-y-6 max-w-[65ch]">
          <p>My own interests have also taken me some way into contemplative practice. Meditation, retreat and periods of traditional study in India have been a significant part of my life for a number of years. I do not assume that counselling should be spiritual, and many of the people I work with have no particular interest in contemplative practice at all. What the background has given me is another set of lenses for understanding attention, suffering, identity, habit, and the ways people relate to their own experience.</p>

          <p>I am wary of reducing a person to a single framework. Psychology illuminates some things very well. Relationships, family history, behaviour, culture, the body, values and meaning may illuminate others. Good counselling often means moving between those perspectives without forcing the person into any one of them.</p>

          <p class="ctc-emph">What matters to me is that the work stays grounded in the actual life of the person in front of me.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-3 mt-10 pt-8 border-t border-parchment/15 font-body text-sm">
          <a href="/about/" class="ctc-link text-parchment/80">More about Michael and Sangham &rarr;</a>
